          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have just been diagnosed with type 2 diabetes. What does the diagnosis mean, what does guidance describe as first-line therapy, and which code covers it?",
          "answer": "A diagnosis of type 2 diabetes means that your body's tissues are not responding well to insulin, a condition called insulin resistance. Your pancreas then struggles to produce enough insulin to manage your blood glucose, causing your blood sugar levels to remain higher than normal. This condition usually develops over several years (Source: condition-type-2-diabetes).\n\nFor first-line therapy, current guidelines recommend that the choice of treatment depends on whether other health conditions are present. If there are no other compelling conditions, metformin, along with structured lifestyle changes, is typically the initial medication. Metformin is used because it has a long history of use, is inexpensive, does not usually cause weight gain, and carries a low risk of causing low blood sugar on its own. Your kidney function needs to be checked before starting and regularly while taking metformin (Source: guideline-type-2-diabetes-first-line-therapy). Guidance also suggests that if your body weight is above a healthy range, losing weight can improve glucose control, blood pressure, and lipid levels. A sustained weight reduction of five percent offers measurable benefits, and greater reductions can even lead to remission in some individuals, particularly earlier in the condition's course. Remission means achieving normal glucose levels without medication, but it is not a cure and requires ongoing monitoring (Source: lifestyle-type-2-diabetes-diet).\n\nIn terms of medical coding, type 2 diabetes mellitus is classified as E11 in the ICD-10-CM system. This code is found in Chapter 4, which covers endocrine, nutritional, and metabolic diseases. If a medical record simply states \"diabetes mellitus\" without specifying the type, it is usually coded as type 2. Using insulin does not change the E11 classification for type 2 diabetes (Source: coding-type-2-diabetes-e11).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
